EDGECLAW(1)                General Commands Manual                EDGECLAW(1)

NAME
     edgeclaw — AI-powered personal assistant agent

SYNOPSIS
     edgeclaw [--setup] [--sandbox] [--help] [--version]

DESCRIPTION
     EdgeClaw is a command-line AI assistant that integrates LLMs, tools, and
     a shell-like task management system.  It supports multi-turn conversa-
     tions, tool calling, and Unix-inspired process control for managing mul-
     tiple parallel agent tasks.

OPTIONS
     -s, --setup    Run initial setup wizard and exit.
     -S, --sandbox  Enable filesystem sandbox for tool execution.
     -h, --help     Display help and exit.
     -V, --version  Output version information and exit.

GETTING STARTED
     Launch edgeclaw to start an interactive CLI session:

           $ edgeclaw

     Type your message and press Enter.  EdgeClaw will respond using the con-
     figured LLM.  The conversation is saved automatically.

TASK MANAGEMENT
     EdgeClaw implements a Unix-like process model for managing multiple AI
     tasks concurrently:

     Turn    — A single request/response exchange (user → LLM → reply).
               Roughly equivalent to a Unix thread.

     Task    — A conversation task with its own context, state, and output.
               Like a Unix process with PID.

     Group   — A collection of related tasks sharing context.  Signals
               sent to the group leader propagate to all members.  Like a
               Unix process group (PGID).

     Session — A login session containing multiple groups.  Like a Unix
               login session.

TASK LIFECYCLE
     A task moves through these states:

       IDLE → WAITING_DEPS → WAITING_INPUT → LLM_PROCESSING
         → TOOL_CALLING → SUSPENDED → COMPLETED / CANCELLED / ERROR

     - COMPLETED tasks auto-reap after replying.
     - SUSPENDED tasks can be resumed.
     - CANCELLED / ERROR tasks can be reaped with /task join.

SHELL-LIKE JOB CONTROL
     EdgeClaw commands mirror Unix shell job control:

     Shell            EdgeClaw
     ─────────────────────────────────────────
     cmd &            /task new "desc" --bg
     jobs             /task list
     fg %1            /task fg <task_id>
     bg %1            /task bg
     kill %1          /task stop <task_id>
     Ctrl+Z           /task suspend <task_id>
     fg / bg (resume) /task resume <task_id>
     cmd1 | cmd2      /task chain <src> <dst>
     pthread_join()   /task join <task_id>

COMMAND REFERENCE
     Session commands
     /new          Start a new session (clear turns, trigger compress).
     /stop         Cancel all active tasks in foreground group.
     /help         Show help message.
     /exit         Exit edgeclaw.

     Task creation
     /task new <desc>         Create a new task and set as foreground.
     /task new <desc> --bg    Create a new background task.
     /task fork <parent_id> <desc> [--prompt <name>]
                              Fork a child task inheriting parent context.

     Task inspection
     /task list               List all tasks grouped by process group.
     /task status [task_id]   Show task details (state, output, deps).
     /task output <task_id>   Show only the task's output text.
     /task groups             List all process groups.

     Foreground / background
     /task fg <task_id>       Bring task's group to foreground (receive input).
     /task bg                 Move current foreground group to background.
     /task switch <task_id>   Switch active task within the foreground group.

     Task control
     /task stop <task_id>     Cancel a task (SIGTERM — graceful, notifies deps).
     /task stop --force <id>  Force-kill a task (SIGKILL — no dep notification).
     /task stop --group <id>  Cancel all tasks in a process group.
     /task suspend <task_id>  Pause a task (Ctrl+Z).
     /task resume <task_id>   Resume a suspended task.

     Dependencies and pipelines
     /task chain <src> <dst>  Make dst wait for src to complete.  When src
                              finishes, its output is injected into dst's
                              LLM context as a system message.  Like a Unix
                              pipe: src's stdout becomes dst's stdin.
     /task join <task_id>     If COMPLETED: show output and reap the task.
                              If RUNNING: show suggestion to chain/wait.

     Subagent / prompt
     /task prompt <id> <name> Set a subagent prompt for the task.

WORKFLOW EXAMPLES
     Simple conversation
           $ edgeclaw
           > 你好
           Welcome! How can I help?
           > 帮我写一个 Python HTTP server
           Here's a simple HTTP server: ...

     Background analysis
           > /task new "分析日志" --bg
           [task] created task-1 (group task-1) in background
           > /task new "分析前端" --bg
           > /task list
             [*] group task-1 (1 members)    foreground
             [+] group task-2 (1 members)    background

     Pipeline (src → dst)
           > /task new "汇总"
           > /task chain task-1 task-0    ← task-0 waits for task-1
           > /task chain task-2 task-0    ← task-0 waits for both
           > /task list
             [task-0 WAITING_DEPS]: waiting for task-1, task-2
           ... task-1, task-2 finish ...
           > /task switch task-0
           > 请汇总以上两个分析结果
           → LLM sees both task-1 and task-2 outputs injected

     Suspend and resume
           > /task suspend task-3
           task task-3 suspended
           > /task resume task-3
           task task-3 resumed

CONTEXT SHARING
     Tasks within the same group automatically share conversation history.
     Each new task in the group sees all prior turns.

     Cross-group communication uses /task chain, which injects the source
     task's output into the destination task's LLM context when the source
     completes.

FILES
     ~/.edgeclaw/                  EdgeClaw home directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/config.json       Configuration file (LLM API key, model).
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/        Workspace root for tools and files.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/memory/MEMORY.md    Agent memory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/history.jsonl       Conversation history.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/skills/             Custom skill/SKILL.md files.

